    "title": "LSU to honor football legend Doug Moreau at homecoming game - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Keymonte Avery ,\n4 hours ago\nBATON ROUGE, La. (Louisiana First) — An LSU Football legend is to be recognized during the South Carolina matchup for his career with the Tigers.\nAccording to LSU Athletics, Doug Moreau was a tight end, wide receiver and kicker for the Tigers from 1962 to 1965. He will be recognized on the field during the first quarter of the homecoming game.\nMoreau was a three-time letterwinner, helping LSU to a win over Syracuse in the 1965 Sugar Bowl and a victory over Arkansas in the 1966 Cotton Bowl.\nThe athlete then went on to play for the Miami Dolphins in 1966 with 73 catches, 926 yards, and six touchdowns. He played in the NFL for four seasons.\nAfter suiting up on the field, Moreau went on to join the football radio and television broadcast for 52 seasons, beginning in 1972 through 2023.\nAcademically, Moreau earned his Juris Doctor degree from the LSU Law School in 1973 and served as a Baton Rouge judge from 1978-1979, a district judge from 1979 to 1990, and a district attorney from 1991 to 2009.\nMoreau was enshrined in the LSU Athletics Hall of Fame in 1976.\nLatest News Copyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to Louisiana First News.",
